[[:db/retract 17592186124994 :question/answer-fields 17592186122564] [:db/retract 17592186125036 :question/answer-fields 17592186122816] {:db/id 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7fe8ffd2-b435-528e-b48e-7a1540a8e35d" "question-e866909b-8838-5c06-a42c-cac8cb9d71e5" "question-3ce9ec8d-1b85-5d4c-b1b5-270c31bca4d5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124308 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](images/bd/bd2cf58c26a51ae147b3714556631e1df786ae6e56cde01f801d9d93f7c258f5.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f0cfdcd8-92d0-5768-aa59-2dc769427d08" "question-2be7f5e5-3b31-5fb7-85df-a4fc0339bd35"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124309 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](images/15/15e0d0c04e0352356acab8001936031505931dac7eb7afefa1881dd333448e57.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](images/17/178769437e55f1822723ac4cc090efca62aac6be9e3ad9dc8f7971b8a6196484.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124310 :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b395052cc098a6daeb50037d07223b20fb93163c10c31a432c7fb9c9a7804db.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](images/82/82e477164a2708a6f8e7f1142dce3e290d3aa216b5828e0523c16555533f02b7.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ef5b0192-0a59-538e-b7e8-f10e0dd5b78e" "question-e28dac56-d247-5873-8ab6-f1ab402c91a9" "question-e6749d20-907b-5109-a739-bd517d7b2b94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124384 :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c61cc387c58af544cf95641a1c2a28e5412d223fb03a7afe5157ee4eba3902bb.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/76/76d9c82037008323681ec9a0745b39740bf1405c767fc9aaee411cdd1fadde1a.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](images/48/481e6321dd23a8af4c59201685295862cc927731eeec31c122ec62bdf156d179.svg)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fe8ffd2-b435-528e-b48e-7a1540a8e35d" :question/id #uuid "7fe8ffd2-b435-528e-b48e-7a1540a8e35d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113559" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8fe4383-9008-55d2-ae25-49270558cc7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8fe4383-9008-55d2-ae25-49270558cc7d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8fe4383-9008-55d2-ae25-49270558cc7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b210422b-4dd8-58b1-96de-8a7991b12498" :answer-field/choices ["answer-663faa0d-6301-5578-b7ea-57233f7c6d5c" "answer-5629c555-bc0f-56b8-91fc-75e26ae8c2ad" "answer-8ec023e5-6b5c-5e85-8721-6e3a12e6f471" "answer-06f91c85-a6af-54d8-84f8-c73027a4b2f6" "answer-b210422b-4dd8-58b1-96de-8a7991b12498"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-663faa0d-6301-5578-b7ea-57233f7c6d5c" :answer/id #uuid "663faa0d-6301-5578-b7ea-57233f7c6d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5629c555-bc0f-56b8-91fc-75e26ae8c2ad" :answer/id #uuid "5629c555-bc0f-56b8-91fc-75e26ae8c2ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 \\\\ 38\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ec023e5-6b5c-5e85-8721-6e3a12e6f471" :answer/id #uuid "8ec023e5-6b5c-5e85-8721-6e3a12e6f471" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 38\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06f91c85-a6af-54d8-84f8-c73027a4b2f6" :answer/id #uuid "06f91c85-a6af-54d8-84f8-c73027a4b2f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b210422b-4dd8-58b1-96de-8a7991b12498" :answer/id #uuid "b210422b-4dd8-58b1-96de-8a7991b12498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e866909b-8838-5c06-a42c-cac8cb9d71e5" :question/id #uuid "e866909b-8838-5c06-a42c-cac8cb9d71e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-82703" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dda3a792-a5c1-587a-9edb-ce17ef59f2fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dda3a792-a5c1-587a-9edb-ce17ef59f2fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "dda3a792-a5c1-587a-9edb-ce17ef59f2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9968b0a6-1e90-5008-8fc1-0d8dbdcacf08" "answer-85f7a332-11d7-56c1-af54-08f89b7cdbb8" "answer-51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" "answer-bdcaea72-2ad3-5720-8e68-209ab0dd89b8" "answer-19c523e9-7f45-5159-8b99-5f289dc80d0e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9968b0a6-1e90-5008-8fc1-0d8dbdcacf08" :answer/id #uuid "9968b0a6-1e90-5008-8fc1-0d8dbdcacf08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f7a332-11d7-56c1-af54-08f89b7cdbb8" :answer/id #uuid "85f7a332-11d7-56c1-af54-08f89b7cdbb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" :answer/id #uuid "51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdcaea72-2ad3-5720-8e68-209ab0dd89b8" :answer/id #uuid "bdcaea72-2ad3-5720-8e68-209ab0dd89b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19c523e9-7f45-5159-8b99-5f289dc80d0e" :answer/id #uuid "19c523e9-7f45-5159-8b99-5f289dc80d0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ce9ec8d-1b85-5d4c-b1b5-270c31bca4d5" :question/id #uuid "3ce9ec8d-1b85-5d4c-b1b5-270c31bca4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-36500" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b04e8c67-5c25-59a1-9e4d-4e87a86fbc03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b04e8c67-5c25-59a1-9e4d-4e87a86fbc03" :answer-field/id #uuid "b04e8c67-5c25-59a1-9e4d-4e87a86fbc03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78245600-a1f8-5a21-88bd-e06c505e5fc9" "answer-229988f7-658e-531e-a75e-4a633a968a80" "answer-b8fb5f8b-a5f6-5906-85ff-1e2f8a50b96f" "answer-8d3b91f8-ceab-5fd2-bfae-9dc1cd0ce79f" "answer-61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78245600-a1f8-5a21-88bd-e06c505e5fc9" :answer/id #uuid "78245600-a1f8-5a21-88bd-e06c505e5fc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-229988f7-658e-531e-a75e-4a633a968a80" :answer/id #uuid "229988f7-658e-531e-a75e-4a633a968a80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8fb5f8b-a5f6-5906-85ff-1e2f8a50b96f" :answer/id #uuid "b8fb5f8b-a5f6-5906-85ff-1e2f8a50b96f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-21 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d3b91f8-ceab-5fd2-bfae-9dc1cd0ce79f" :answer/id #uuid "8d3b91f8-ceab-5fd2-bfae-9dc1cd0ce79f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d" :answer/id #uuid "61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0cfdcd8-92d0-5768-aa59-2dc769427d08" :question/id #uuid "f0cfdcd8-92d0-5768-aa59-2dc769427d08" :question/math-academy-id "q-113597" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2},$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d503694-4c65-5f5e-80a7-c8e1b24fe6d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d503694-4c65-5f5e-80a7-c8e1b24fe6d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d503694-4c65-5f5e-80a7-c8e1b24fe6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ed545a0d-bb95-5b28-bd97-fb2a46aed464" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed545a0d-bb95-5b28-bd97-fb2a46aed464" "answer-67ef9947-0abd-58ce-bfaa-502e27646e6a" "answer-86daf74f-004a-52d1-918a-94714d07c50d" "answer-1a297d16-52a8-53eb-9dad-1a87b66f4056" "answer-87c5730a-1432-52b5-aaf3-ec874082be11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed545a0d-bb95-5b28-bd97-fb2a46aed464" :answer/id #uuid "ed545a0d-bb95-5b28-bd97-fb2a46aed464" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ef9947-0abd-58ce-bfaa-502e27646e6a" :answer/id #uuid "67ef9947-0abd-58ce-bfaa-502e27646e6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86daf74f-004a-52d1-918a-94714d07c50d" :answer/id #uuid "86daf74f-004a-52d1-918a-94714d07c50d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a297d16-52a8-53eb-9dad-1a87b66f4056" :answer/id #uuid "1a297d16-52a8-53eb-9dad-1a87b66f4056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87c5730a-1432-52b5-aaf3-ec874082be11" :answer/id #uuid "87c5730a-1432-52b5-aaf3-ec874082be11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2be7f5e5-3b31-5fb7-85df-a4fc0339bd35" :question/id #uuid "2be7f5e5-3b31-5fb7-85df-a4fc0339bd35" :question/math-academy-id "q-82598" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-259091c3-ae0d-594d-b51a-a8e034136037"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-259091c3-ae0d-594d-b51a-a8e034136037" :answer-field/id #uuid "259091c3-ae0d-594d-b51a-a8e034136037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e45951f9-b2ac-5925-8dca-6a0e9e74ecc4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c1b7743-c305-5999-a721-23098449b1cb" "answer-848c95d9-4948-5e5b-b405-b76d8bb0404e" "answer-76daf001-db03-5b02-b5ce-d1bdb4a9261e" "answer-e5ad6413-b74c-561a-9d52-2593718ee8e9" "answer-e45951f9-b2ac-5925-8dca-6a0e9e74ecc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c1b7743-c305-5999-a721-23098449b1cb" :answer/id #uuid "5c1b7743-c305-5999-a721-23098449b1cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-848c95d9-4948-5e5b-b405-b76d8bb0404e" :answer/id #uuid "848c95d9-4948-5e5b-b405-b76d8bb0404e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76daf001-db03-5b02-b5ce-d1bdb4a9261e" :answer/id #uuid "76daf001-db03-5b02-b5ce-d1bdb4a9261e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5ad6413-b74c-561a-9d52-2593718ee8e9" :answer/id #uuid "e5ad6413-b74c-561a-9d52-2593718ee8e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e45951f9-b2ac-5925-8dca-6a0e9e74ecc4" :answer/id #uuid "e45951f9-b2ac-5925-8dca-6a0e9e74ecc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12" :question/id #uuid "15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12" :question/math-academy-id "q-82713" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61a8f832-f2b3-5123-8233-870da3bb21e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61a8f832-f2b3-5123-8233-870da3bb21e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "61a8f832-f2b3-5123-8233-870da3bb21e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" "answer-fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" "answer-f99a0a3e-8e69-5c5a-8d55-fb74464b4d49" "answer-7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" "answer-c0ab3da6-d52a-52fe-8824-525245387581"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" :answer/id #uuid "339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/id #uuid "fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f99a0a3e-8e69-5c5a-8d55-fb74464b4d49" :answer/id #uuid "f99a0a3e-8e69-5c5a-8d55-fb74464b4d49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" :answer/id #uuid "7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0ab3da6-d52a-52fe-8824-525245387581" :answer/id #uuid "c0ab3da6-d52a-52fe-8824-525245387581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125036 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 16-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59" :answer-field/id #uuid "77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" "answer-d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" "answer-7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" "answer-af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" "answer-fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" :answer/id #uuid "c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/id #uuid "d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" :answer/id #uuid "7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" :answer/id #uuid "af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c" :answer/id #uuid "fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef5b0192-0a59-538e-b7e8-f10e0dd5b78e" :question/id #uuid "ef5b0192-0a59-538e-b7e8-f10e0dd5b78e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82621" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 16 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b4d2688-b184-5811-a90b-5873e7a47367"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b4d2688-b184-5811-a90b-5873e7a47367" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4d2688-b184-5811-a90b-5873e7a47367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e2646f8-d294-5f71-920f-5fb9383c0ce2" "answer-c2f56259-99a1-5f0e-acbf-9e1fb79a1fc7" "answer-8c4a3883-bbae-5787-8a87-5357e57022a9" "answer-3bc312ec-9a5e-5eb2-8bb9-a8936c73c9ce" "answer-00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e2646f8-d294-5f71-920f-5fb9383c0ce2" :answer/id #uuid "4e2646f8-d294-5f71-920f-5fb9383c0ce2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2f56259-99a1-5f0e-acbf-9e1fb79a1fc7" :answer/id #uuid "c2f56259-99a1-5f0e-acbf-9e1fb79a1fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c4a3883-bbae-5787-8a87-5357e57022a9" :answer/id #uuid "8c4a3883-bbae-5787-8a87-5357e57022a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bc312ec-9a5e-5eb2-8bb9-a8936c73c9ce" :answer/id #uuid "3bc312ec-9a5e-5eb2-8bb9-a8936c73c9ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096" :answer/id #uuid "00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e28dac56-d247-5873-8ab6-f1ab402c91a9" :question/id #uuid "e28dac56-d247-5873-8ab6-f1ab402c91a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113612" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ef4b0bd2-0e38-5371-ace1-939725dda131"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ef4b0bd2-0e38-5371-ace1-939725dda131" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4b0bd2-0e38-5371-ace1-939725dda131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c69c82e9-2236-526a-ae72-3c203db881f9" "answer-9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" "answer-ad75aa8f-a4c6-595d-8e09-373c61a5ec3c" "answer-88490a55-b515-5dba-8dd0-a984dd302a75" "answer-3665ab07-2fe3-53da-8bd4-219390934492"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c69c82e9-2236-526a-ae72-3c203db881f9" :answer/id #uuid "c69c82e9-2236-526a-ae72-3c203db881f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer/id #uuid "9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad75aa8f-a4c6-595d-8e09-373c61a5ec3c" :answer/id #uuid "ad75aa8f-a4c6-595d-8e09-373c61a5ec3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88490a55-b515-5dba-8dd0-a984dd302a75" :answer/id #uuid "88490a55-b515-5dba-8dd0-a984dd302a75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3665ab07-2fe3-53da-8bd4-219390934492" :answer/id #uuid "3665ab07-2fe3-53da-8bd4-219390934492" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6749d20-907b-5109-a739-bd517d7b2b94" :question/id #uuid "e6749d20-907b-5109-a739-bd517d7b2b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-82717" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c43a46c-e182-5838-aaf6-58e012185c17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c43a46c-e182-5838-aaf6-58e012185c17" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c43a46c-e182-5838-aaf6-58e012185c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf4e4798-2fc8-5413-93d4-21fffdbf8a55" "answer-4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" "answer-164c778b-5b14-5603-bf90-827e8830f635" "answer-478f5fce-6266-5067-9a1b-4f2ed288f0d0" "answer-f2cec5d4-fb73-55ac-9a1a-3d5575086234"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf4e4798-2fc8-5413-93d4-21fffdbf8a55" :answer/id #uuid "bf4e4798-2fc8-5413-93d4-21fffdbf8a55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" :answer/id #uuid "4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-164c778b-5b14-5603-bf90-827e8830f635" :answer/id #uuid "164c778b-5b14-5603-bf90-827e8830f635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-478f5fce-6266-5067-9a1b-4f2ed288f0d0" :answer/id #uuid "478f5fce-6266-5067-9a1b-4f2ed288f0d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2cec5d4-fb73-55ac-9a1a-3d5575086234" :answer/id #uuid "f2cec5d4-fb73-55ac-9a1a-3d5575086234" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125822 :tutorial/content "We've seen that to find the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to a basis $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, the formula looks as follows:\n![](images/89/890f34433211786e2cb8aa71057de40bdf3b12678c2bbb5a79ef5e680555937f.svg)\n\nAlso, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n\t\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125708 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Now, suppose we would like to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ \n\n- We first need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+x_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_1,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/58/58010a2a5206e3fc27e3d924de70cf32dcc92c3578bdb2821ace7278fbb223cf.svg)\n\n- Similarly, we need to find $y_1$ and $y_2$ such that \n\n$$\ny_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+y_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/68/68788aa3e388c5cba14662a947c1b7afaacb95f611c256d11a7a6d7614c352b9.svg)\n\nWe can solve both systems using Gaussian elimination. However, we can do that simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/4b/4b00a4cbd7831f6da6acd06a087d5453bd455a89eb81f6b3036b1b572bb064d1.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get a matrix of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {x}_{1} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}_{2} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the required coefficients will be stored in the columns on the right-hand side." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124710 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
